\addvspace {10pt}
\addvspace {10pt}
\addvspace {10pt}
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces The signal-and-annotation legend. Solid arrows move forward; dashed arrows return feedback; every cognitive net is typed or explicitly named.}}{23}{figure.3.1}%
\addvspace {10pt}
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Flow and state-retention symbols. The short memory line states the preserved functional role; ENG marks a stipulated cognitive quantity.}}{29}{figure.4.1}%
\addvspace {10pt}
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Direction, threshold, conversion, and sensing symbols. The bridge rectifier is intentionally limited to a scalar-polarity teaching analogy; it does not convert Negative into Positive.}}{37}{figure.5.1}%
\addvspace {10pt}
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Active and comparative parts. Cause, Bias, and Effect are cognitive terminal roles on the CJT; they are not claims that a standard BJT's collector, base, and emitter have those signal roles in every configuration.}}{46}{figure.6.1}%
\addvspace {10pt}
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.1}{\ignorespaces Rhythm and conversion parts. Pure ADC/DAC register examples keep Noetic and Domain fixed; a Domain change requires a separate directed crossing or conversion stage.}}{53}{figure.7.1}%
\addvspace {10pt}
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.1}{\ignorespaces Logic and routing symbols. Boolean truth rules apply only after continuous CAS activations have been converted into explicit firing flags.}}{59}{figure.8.1}%
\addvspace {10pt}
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.1}{\ignorespaces Regulation, protection, and annotation symbols. A fuse threshold must be below every upstream clamp or saturation ceiling that feeds it, or the fuse is unreachable.}}{69}{figure.9.1}%
